{"id":"7c76f16f-b8d8-46cd-8b62-39f08f1c0152","object":"chat.completion","created":1785037304,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if nodes is None or required_symbols is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must contain 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes must be list, required_symbols must be list\\\")\\n    node_dict = {}\\n    parent_to_children = {}\\n    all_symbols = set()\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if not isinstance(node, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"each node must be a dict\\\")\\n        nid = node.get('id')\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        symbol = node.get('symbol')\\n        depth = node.get('depth')\\n        removable = node.get('removable')\\n        utility = node.get('utility')\\n        if nid is None or parent is None or symbol is None or depth is None or removable is None or utility is None:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"node missing required fields\\\")\\n        if nid in node_dict:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"duplicate node id\\\")\\n        node_dict[nid] = node\\n        if parent not in parent_to_children:\\n            parent_to_children[parent] = []\\n        parent_to_children[parent].append(nid)\\n        all_symbols.add(symbol)\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"required symbol not present in any node\\\")\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    def required_symbols_retained(exclude_id=None):\\n        present = set()\\n        for n in nodes:\\n            nid = n['id']\\n            if nid in removed_set or nid == exclude_id:\\n                continue\\n            present.add(n['symbol'])\\n        return all(sym in present for sym in required_symbols)\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        children = parent_to_children.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained_children = any(cid not in removed_set for cid in children)\\n        if has_retained_children:\\n            continue\\n        if required_symbols_retained(exclude_id=nid):\\n            removed_set.add(nid)\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set])\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_node_ids}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a Python function `solve(case)` that implements grammar-guided node reduction based on the given description. The case payload contains nodes (a list of dicts) with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The goal is to remove some nodes (only those with removable=True) while preserving that all required_symbols are present in the remaining nodes, and presumably that the tree remains valid (children of removed nodes? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to decide which removable nodes to remove.\n\nWe need to output removed_node_ids (list of strings) and retained_node_ids (list of strings). The ordering: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted, removed_node_ids in the order they were removed. Also \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So depth descending. Use node ID as tie breaker at equal depth. Also \"registered_candidate_order\" may refer to the order of removal candidates? But we are not given a list of candidates; we need to generate candidates from nodes that are removable.\n\nInterpretation: We have a tree of nodes. Each node has parent (null for root). removable flag indicates if it can be removed. But we cannot remove if it has retained children? \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node is removed, its children must also be removed or reattached? The phrase \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" suggests maybe we can remove a node and replace it with its children? But the description is simplified to removal only? Let's read carefully: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But later: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So removal means removing a node and its subtree? Or just removing the node and connecting its children to its parent? Usually in Perses (a known tool), they do \"deletion\" where a node is removed and its children are reattached to its parent. But here \"node with retained children\" suggests that if a node has children that are not removed, we cannot remove the node because that would orphan them. So we need to decide for each removable node whether to remove it (and its subtree?) or not.\n\nGiven \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" suggests processing in depth order. For each node (starting from deepest), decide if it can be removed. But removal of a node might affect deeper nodes (its descendants). Since we process deep first, we can remove leaf nodes first. But the condition \"node with retained children\" means if a node has children that are not removed, we cannot remove it. So if we process deep first, a leaf node (no children) can be removed if it's removable and not a required symbol. Then when we consider its parent, if all children have been removed, then we can consider removing the parent? But the parent might have other children retained. So the algorithm: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id as tiebreaker. For each node in that order, if it's removable, and it's not the root, and its symbol is not in required_symbols, and all its children (if any) have been removed (so that removing it would not orphan retained children), and after tentatively removing it, all required symbols are still present in the remaining tree, then remove it. But we need to check required symbols globally: after removal, all required symbols must still appear somewhere. Since we process deep first, we can keep a set of removed node ids. Also need to update the tree? Not exactly, we just need to decide removal. The condition \"children have been removed\" can be checked: a node's children are those nodes whose parent equals its id. If any such child is not in removed set, then node cannot be removed.\n\nWe need to output removed_node_ids in removal order (the order we remove them). retained_node_ids are those not removed, sorted by id (or maybe by some order? \"Return sorted retained IDs\" so alphabetical or numeric? Since ids are strings, sort them lexicographically.\n\nAlso \"Never remove the root\" – root has id \"root\" and parent null, also removable false. So we skip root.\n\nAdditionally, \"required_symbols\" are symbols that must remain present in the tree after removal. Check that every symbol in required_symbols is present in at least one retained node. Also the root symbol is always present.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse nodes into dict by id. Build mapping from parent to children list.\n2. Build a set of required symbols.\n3. Initialize removed_set = set(), removed_order = [].\n4. Sort nodes by (depth descending, id). Since depth is given, we can sort.\n5. For each node in that order:\n   - Check if node['removable'] is True.\n   - Skip if node['id'] == 'root' (or node['parent'] is None? But root has removable false anyway).\n   - Skip if node['symbol'] in required_symbols.\n   - Check children: if any child (from mapping) is not in removed_set, then skip (because node has retained children).\n   - Simulate removal: check if after removing this node, all required symbols still exist. We can compute current required symbols presence: first get all retained nodes (those not in removed_set and not this node). Check if each required symbol appears in those nodes. If yes, then remove.\n   - If conditions met, add to removed_set and append node['id'] to removed_order.\n6. After processing all nodes, retained_node_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set]).\n7. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nNote: The order of processing deep first ensures that we consider leaf nodes first. However, there might be multiple nodes at same depth and same symbol etc. The tie breaker is node id (string). Use stable sort.\n\nEdge cases: If removal of a node causes a required symbol to be absent, we skip that node. This iterative process might not consider later possibilities; but the algorithm is greedy in order defined.\n\nAlso \"grammar_valid\" might impose additional constraints about the tree shape after removal, but not specified. Probably just not removing required symbols and root and children.\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) that returns this dict. Also raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. Infeasible could be if required_symbols contain a symbol not present in any node? But we can check that initially. If some required symbol is not present, raise ValueError. Also if root is missing? Assumed present.\n\nAlso note \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check that case has 'nodes' and 'required_symbols', each node has required fields id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation in Python: Use standard library only.\n\nWe need to produce a single JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code as a string. Must escape newlines etc.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate case structure\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be a dict\")\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\n    if nodes is None or required_symbols is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"case must contain 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'\")\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"nodes must be list, required_symbols must be list\")\n    # Build node dict and validate each node\n    node_dict = {}\n    parent_to_children = {}\n    all_symbols = set()\n    for node in nodes:\n        if not isinstance(node, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"each node must be a dict\")\n        nid = node.get('id')\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        symbol = node.get('symbol')\n        depth = node.get('depth')\n        removable = node.get('removable')\n        utility = node.get('utility')\n        if nid is None or parent is None or symbol is None or depth is None or removable is None or utility is None:\n            raise ValueError(\"node missing required fields\")\n        if nid in node_dict:\n            raise ValueError(\"duplicate node id\")\n        node_dict[nid] = node\n        if parent not in parent_to_children:\n            parent_to_children[parent] = []\n        parent_to_children[parent].append(nid)\n        all_symbols.add(symbol)\n    # Check required symbols exist\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in all_symbols:\n            raise ValueError(\"required symbol not present in any node\")\n    \n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    removed_set = set()\n    removed_order = []\n    \n    # Function to check if all required symbols are present in retained nodes (excluding removed_set and possibly a candidate)\n    def required_symbols_retained(exclude_id=None):\n        present = set()\n        for n in nodes:\n            nid = n['id']\n            if nid in removed_set or nid == exclude_id:\n                continue\n            present.add(n['symbol'])\n        return all(sym in present for sym in required_symbols)\n    \n    # Initially check feasibility (if we removed everything, can't retain required)\n    # That is fine, we'll check per removal.\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        # Skip root, non-removable, required symbol\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == 'root':  # also root has removable false, but check anyway\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check children retained: any child not in removed_set?\n        children = parent_to_children.get(nid, [])\n        has_retained_children = any(cid not in removed_set for cid in children)\n        if has_retained_children:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols after removal\n        if required_symbols_retained(exclude_id=nid):\n            removed_set.add(nid)\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_node_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set])\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids\n    }\n```\n\nBut note: The condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" means if any child is retained (not